    {
      "accepted_because": "This minor wording coloring ('stone buildings' for tot aedificia following \u00a75's destructae domus, atque Ecclesiae a fundamentis eversae sunt) stands in settled prose untouched in iteration 3, does not mislead the reader about Gregory's argument or scene, and does not justify a further repair round.",
      "id": "STU-011",
      "location": "sections/04-lane-eschatological.tex:16",
      "problem": "In summarizing St. Gregory the Great's reflection on the Roman windstorm (Homiliae in Evangelia 1.6), line 16 asks, 'If the invisible Judge can overturn stone buildings by stirring a single breath of wind and a thin cloud, Gregory asks, what will happen when He appears in person?' Direct verification of the bound Wikisource Latin witness (gregory-whole.txt, line 19, Hom. 1.6) shows Gregory writes 'casura tot aedificiorum fundamenta concussit' and 'tot aedificia stravit' ('shook the foundations of so many buildings' / 'leveled so many buildings') without specifying 'stone'.",
      "required_result": "If sections/04-lane-eschatological.tex:16 is reopened in a future edit, replace 'overturn stone buildings' with 'overturn so many buildings' or 'shake the foundations of so many buildings' to match Gregory's Latin wording.",
      "severity": "accepted"
    }
  ],
