  "summary": "Independent cold review of the Gemini 1962 Roman Rite First Sunday of Advent expansive study (src/gemini/liturgy/roman-rite/1962/propers/temporal/01-first-sunday-of-advent/main.tex, sections/01-opening.tex through sections/09-references.tex, proper-components.toml, and publication.toml) returns PASS with zero blocking findings and one accepted minor wording note (STU-011). Cross-checked appointed_text_completeness = \"complete\" against guidance/liturgy/propers-production-plan.md (authorized 2026-11-25, no reference-only scope), research/context.md, research/scope.md, propers/verified.md, and sections/02-appointed-text.tex; verified strict 1962 liturgical isolation, carrying-author standing in src/sources/inventories/author-standing-v1.toml across all three interpretations (7, 3, and 5 registered Fathers/Doctors/Blesseds respectively, with St. Bernard kept outside carrying_authors), and read-only preflight/component/document-type checks (all 12 preflight checks, check-proper-components, and document-types refresh --check exited 0). Verified the REVIEW_SCOPE upstream synchronization (sections/01, 04, 07, 09) directly against the tracked witnesses in src/sources/: St. Augustine, De civitate Dei XX.14 (NPNF I.2 ccel-text-5c973fed lines 37271-37339), XX.16 (lines 37406-37450), and XX.24 (lines 38160-38338), Clementine Vulgate Ps. 101:26-28 and Apoc. 20:9-13, and St. Bede, In Lucae Evangelium Expositio VI on Luke 21:28-29, 32-33 (luke-21-checked-cd9a5956), plus spot-checks of St. Gregory (Hom. 1.1-6 Wikisource Latin, Bute 1908, Oxford 1843), St. Cyril of Alexandria (Sermon CXXXIX, Payne Smith pp. 653-654), St. Ambrose (Exp. in Luc. X.37-45), St. John Chrysostom (Hom. in Rom. 24), St. Robert Bellarmine (Ps. 24, 79, 84), St. Augustine (Enarr. in Ps. 84.15 Coxe), St. Bernard (Missus est Hom. 1 p. 59; In Adventu 2 p. 19, 3.4, 4.1, 5.1), St. Leo (Serm. 12.4, 17.1, 19.1), Bl. Ildefonso Schuster (Sacramentary I pp. 321-323), William Durandus (Rationale VI.2-3 fols. 256r-v, 261r-v), John Beleth (Summa LXIV), and Cornelius a Lapide (Rom. 13:12-13, pp. 196-197). Consequential-difficulty test: (1) Luke 21:33 at Bede luke-21-33.toml, Augustine De civ. Dei XX.14/16/24 (ccel-text-5c973fed), and Gregory Hom. 1.4 (gregory-whole.txt line 18) \u2014 PASS: sections/04-lane-eschatological.tex:22-24, 46 and sections/01:6, 07:8, 09:22 teach the paradox and Eccl. 1:4 / 2 Pet. 3:5-7 resolution clearly, quote Augustine verbatim, and keep Bede's direct Luke 21:33 synthesis distinct from Augustine's three Book XX chapters without claiming Augustine expounds Luke 21:33; (2) Luke 21:27-30 at Gregory 1.2-3, Cyril Sermon CXXXIX, and Ambrose X.37-45 \u2014 PASS: real patristic divergence (fructus mundi ruina est vs. bodily resurrection vs. Marian cloud and blossoming nations with duplex figura) is clearly argued; (3) Ps. 84:13 at Bellarmine psalm-84-verse-13.toml, Bernard Missus est Hom. 1 p. 59 / In Adventu 2 p. 19, and Augustine psalm-84-15.toml \u2014 PASS: Old Latin suavitatem vs. Gallican benignitatem is disclosed and each inference is intelligible. Prose across sections/01-09 meets guidance/editorial.md plain-prose and tradition-inhabiting standards without production jargon, closing roll calls, compositional-intent claims, or authored biblical dates contradicting generated/scriptural-date-location.tex. Limits: this stage reviews canonical study sources and evidence only (no PDF visual review or canonical web inspection); downstream concise-study and homily re-synchronization remain for their own stages, and offline remote/restricted holdings disclosed in sections/08-scope.tex remain uninspected."
